WI-havit: every bakeoff script that turns a cohort repo NAME into a PATH now goes
through the shared resolver.

THE BUG. `bakeoff-deep run --pool ~/ALL_REPOS` -- the documented usage, and the
only pool on the lab machine -- died with FileNotFoundError for any repo living
one level down in a collection subdir:

  $ ./scripts/bakeoff-deep cohort --repos django
    django   56.3MB  6915 files       <- SELECTION resolves it, and stores the path
  $ ./scripts/bakeoff-deep run
    FileNotFoundError: '/home/jgstern_agent/ALL_REPOS/django'   <- RUN re-derives it

Cohort metadata already held `"path": ".../whole_bunch_of_repos/django"`.
`_run_cohort_repos` discarded it and rebuilt a weaker one.

THE INVARIANT: a path already resolved by the shared resolver must not be
re-derived downstream. Selection and run must agree BY CONSTRUCTION.

THE FIX ALREADY EXISTED IN THE SIBLING. WI-favav added `_pool_repo_path` to
`bakeoff-broad` for this exact symptom; `bakeoff-deep` already imports the
resolver and cites WI-favav in its selection path. Only the run path missed it.

ENUMERATED THE FAMILY INSTEAD OF PATCHING THE LINE THAT FAILED -- three more
sites, and the worst is inside `bakeoff-deep cycle`:

  scripts/bakeoff-deep:1980          run loop            <- crashed
  scripts/bakeoff-deep-reflect:893   reflect prompts     <- SILENT
  scripts/bakeoff-broad-reflect:688  reflect prompts     <- SILENT
  both reflect scripts               agent-facing guidance string

The reflect sites do not crash. They hand the reflect agent a source path that
does not exist, so a cycle can select and analyse repos correctly and then
verdict them on absence. A crash is the good failure mode.

TESTS SPLIT PER SCRIPT ON PURPOSE. A single family-wide file passed locally and
would have passed CI here, then never run again -- the top-level test map selects
root tests by name prefix and no name maps to four scripts. Its own guard
(`test_top_level_test_map.py`) caught it. Naming each file after the script it
covers was the honest fix; `KNOWN_UNREACHABLE` exists for tests the mapper
structurally cannot claim, which is not this. Each copy carries the family-wide
enumeration check, so touching any one script re-runs it across all four.

